(() => {
  window.lessonBank = window.lessonBank || {};

  const add = (id, lesson) => { window.lessonBank[id] = lesson; };

  add('econ1', {
    unit:'ECON1000', date:'20–26 Jul', week:'1', duration:'120–180 min',
    title:'L1 · Strategic Interactions and Economic Outcomes (I)',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin ECON1000 L1 Strategic Interactions and Economic Outcomes (I) lecture deck plus the S2 2026 L0–L2 Student Tutorial Guide and its social-dilemma, climate-change and repeated-game activities.',
    objectives:[
      'Recognise a social dilemma and explain why individually sensible choices can create a worse collective outcome.',
      'Identify players, strategies, information and payoffs in a game.',
      'Read a payoff matrix and find best responses, dominant strategies and Nash equilibria.',
      'Explain why self-interest sometimes produces good outcomes and sometimes produces a prisoner’s dilemma.',
      'Explain how social preferences, repeated interaction, norms and punishment can change behaviour.',
      'Apply game theory to climate, public-good and coordination problems.'
    ],
    map:['social interaction','players + strategies','payoffs','best responses','equilibrium','social dilemma','institutions / preferences'],
    sections:[
      {kicker:'big picture',title:'1. Economics here is about interdependence, not isolated choice',body:[
        'In this first block the question is not simply “what would I choose?” It is “what should I choose when my result depends on what somebody else chooses too?” That is a strategic interaction.',
        'A social interaction involves more than one person or party and one person’s action affects other people’s outcomes. A strategic interaction is the more specific case where people understand that interdependence and choose while thinking about the other side.',
        'This matters because many economic problems—competition, bargaining, climate policy, vaccination, workplace cooperation and public goods—cannot be understood by looking at one decision-maker in isolation.'
      ],checkpoint:'I can distinguish an ordinary choice from a strategic interaction.'},
      {kicker:'social dilemmas',title:'2. When individually sensible behaviour creates a bad group outcome',body:[
        'A social dilemma is a situation where independently self-interested actions lead to a socially suboptimal outcome. Traffic congestion and climate change are examples from the lecture.',
        'The tension is not that people are irrational. The tension is that the rules and incentives of the situation can make a privately attractive action harmful when many people make the same choice.',
        'This is why public policy is part of the topic. Quotas, taxes and locally created institutions can change incentives or rules so that the strategic outcome changes.'
      ],bullets:['Quotas can restrict over-exploitation of a common resource.','Taxes can make socially costly behaviour privately more expensive.','Communities can create institutions and enforcement rules themselves.'],checkpoint:'I can explain why a social dilemma can exist even when everyone acts rationally.'},
      {kicker:'game anatomy',title:'3. The four things you must identify before solving a game',body:[
        'A game is a simplified model of a social interaction. Curtin’s lecture builds it from four pieces: players, feasible strategies, information and payoffs.',
        'Players are the decision-makers. Strategies are the actions available to each player. Information is what each player knows when choosing. Payoffs summarise how desirable each possible outcome is to each player.',
        'Do not rush to equilibrium before identifying these parts. Most game-theory mistakes begin because the student has not been clear about who is choosing what.'
      ],flow:['players','strategies','information','payoffs','solve the interaction'],checkpoint:'I can set up a game before trying to solve it.'},
      {kicker:'payoff matrices',title:'4. Reading a payoff matrix properly',body:[
        'A payoff matrix lists the payoff to each player for every possible combination of strategies. The exact ordering convention must be read carefully—usually one player’s payoff is written first and the other player’s second.',
        'To find a best response for the row player, hold the column player’s strategy fixed and compare the row player’s available payoffs. Then repeat for every possible column action. Do the symmetric exercise for the column player.',
        'A best response is conditional: it is the best action given what the other player does. A dominant strategy is stronger: it is the best response no matter what the other player does.'
      ],bullets:['Best response = best action given the other player’s action.','Dominant strategy = best response to every action the other player could take.','Dominant-strategy equilibrium = everyone plays a dominant strategy.'],practicePrompt:'Take any 2×2 matrix from your notes. Cover one player’s payoffs and underline the other player’s best response in each case.',checkpoint:'I can identify best responses without guessing from the total payoff.'},
      {kicker:'self-interest can work',title:'5. When the “invisible hand” gives a good outcome',body:[
        'The crop-choice example shows a case where each farmer pursuing the action that maximises their own payoff can also produce an outcome that is attractive for both players and large in total payoff.',
        'The lesson is not “self-interest is always good.” It is that institutional structure and payoffs matter. In some games, private incentives line up with mutual gains. In others, they do not.',
        'Economic analysis therefore asks what the rules and incentives generate, not whether self-interest is morally good or bad in the abstract.'
      ],checkpoint:'I can explain why the same behavioural assumption can produce very different social outcomes in different games.'},
      {kicker:'prisoner’s dilemma',title:'6. When dominant strategies make everyone worse off',body:[
        'A prisoner’s dilemma is the classic case where each player has a dominant strategy, yet when both follow it, both receive a lower payoff than under another feasible outcome.',
        'Curtin’s pesticide example has both farmers choosing the privately attractive strategy, producing an equilibrium where both receive less than if both had chosen the cooperative alternative.',
        'This is the central logic of a social dilemma: the equilibrium can be predictable and individually rational while still being collectively poor.'
      ],flow:['private incentive','dominant strategy','both choose it','equilibrium','mutually better outcome left unrealised'],checkpoint:'I can tell the difference between “equilibrium” and “best feasible outcome”.'},
      {kicker:'nash equilibrium',title:'7. Nash equilibrium when there is no dominant strategy',body:[
        'Many games do not contain dominant strategies. Nash equilibrium gives a broader concept: a set of strategies where each player’s chosen strategy is a best response to everyone else’s chosen strategies.',
        'A game can have one Nash equilibrium, several, or none in pure strategies. Curtin uses coordination examples such as which side of the road to drive on, where more than one coordinated outcome can be self-enforcing.',
        'Multiple equilibria raise a practical question: which one will society coordinate on? History, norms, expectations, policy and bargaining can all matter.'
      ],checkpoint:'I can test a candidate outcome by asking whether either player would want to change unilaterally.'},
      {kicker:'public goods',title:'8. Free riding, social preferences and repeated interaction',body:[
        'A public good is non-rival: one person’s use does not reduce its availability to others. The lecture’s irrigation example illustrates the free-rider problem. Contributing has a private cost, while benefits spill across the group.',
        'If people care only about their own monetary payoff in a one-shot game, non-contribution can become dominant. Experiments, however, show that behaviour is not captured by narrow self-interest alone.',
        'Curtin emphasises altruism, reciprocity and inequality aversion. In repeated games, future consequences, social norms and peer punishment can sustain more cooperation because today’s selfish action can affect how others treat you tomorrow.'
      ],bullets:['Altruism: willingness to help another person at a cost to yourself.','Reciprocity: desire to reward good behaviour and punish bad behaviour.','Inequality aversion: dislike of unequal outcomes.'],checkpoint:'I can explain why repeated interaction can change the predicted behaviour from a one-shot game.'},
      {kicker:'application',title:'9. Climate change and herd immunity as strategic problems',body:[
        'The tutorial asks you to model climate policy as a game between large actors with choices such as restricting emissions versus business as usual. The environmental benefit is shared, creating an incentive to free ride on another country’s costly emissions reductions.',
        'The herd-immunity activity has similar structure. Whether avoiding vaccination is attractive depends partly on how many other people vaccinate. The private payoff therefore changes with the behaviour of the group.',
        'The purpose is not to memorise one matrix. It is to practise translating a messy real-world problem into players, strategies, payoffs and incentives, then asking whether the predicted equilibrium creates a social dilemma.'
      ],checkpoint:'I can construct a plausible game from a real-world coordination problem and justify the payoffs.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'What is the difference between a best response and a dominant strategy?',answer:'A best response is optimal given a particular action by the other player. A dominant strategy is a best response to every possible action of the other player.'},
      {q:'Why can a prisoner’s-dilemma equilibrium be socially undesirable even though each player is acting rationally?',answer:'Because each player’s private incentive makes the dominant strategy optimal individually, but when both choose it the resulting payoffs are lower than a feasible cooperative outcome.'},
      {q:'In a repeated public-goods game, why might contributions remain higher than in a one-shot game?',answer:'Because reciprocity, social norms, reputation and the possibility of peer punishment make current behaviour affect future payoffs and treatment.'},
      {q:'Climate mitigation creates benefits that spill across countries. What strategic problem does that create?',answer:'A free-rider problem: each country may prefer others to bear the cost of reducing emissions while it receives part of the shared climate benefit.'}
    ],
    finish:['I can set up and solve a 2×2 payoff matrix.','I can identify best responses, dominant strategies and Nash equilibria.','I can explain a prisoner’s dilemma and free riding.','I can explain how social preferences and repeated games change outcomes.','I attempted the L1 tutorial applications rather than only rereading definitions.']
  });

  add('econ2', {
    unit:'ECON1000', date:'27 Jul–9 Aug', week:'2–3', duration:'120–180 min',
    title:'L2 · Strategic Interactions and Economic Outcomes (II)',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin ECON1000 L2 lecture deck plus the L2(a)/L2(b) workshop guide activities on efficiency, fairness, sequential games, panic buying, social media, strikes and the GTP-style worked application.',
    objectives:['Analyse sequential games using a game tree.','Distinguish an allocation from the process that produced it.','Apply the Pareto criterion to efficiency.','Separate Nash equilibrium from Pareto efficiency.','Explain substantive and procedural fairness.','Use preferences, power and rules of the game to evaluate outcomes.','Apply game theory in the style expected by the GTP.'],
    map:['allocation','game tree','backward reasoning','Nash outcome','Pareto test','fairness test','policy / rule change'],
    sections:[
      {kicker:'allocation',title:'1. Start by asking what outcome the interaction produces',body:['An allocation is the outcome of an economic interaction. In L2, Curtin moves beyond predicting behaviour and asks how we should evaluate the result.','Two major evaluation lenses are efficiency and fairness. They are not the same thing, and a policy can improve one while worsening the other.'],checkpoint:'I can describe an allocation without immediately calling it fair or efficient.'},
      {kicker:'sequential games',title:'2. Game trees: when players move in sequence',body:['A simultaneous payoff matrix is not always the right representation. When one player moves first and another observes that move before responding, use a game tree.','The ultimatum game is the core example. A proposer chooses how to divide a prize, then the responder accepts or rejects. Rejection can destroy value for both players.','Sequential structure changes the strategic problem because later players condition their actions on what they observe earlier.'],flow:['first mover chooses','second mover observes','second mover responds','payoffs realised'],checkpoint:'I can recognise when a problem should be represented as a game tree rather than a simultaneous matrix.'},
      {kicker:'ultimatum game',title:'3. Why narrow self-interest predicts one thing but experiments often show another',body:['If the responder cared only about their own monetary payoff, any positive offer would be better than zero and should be accepted.','In experiments, very low offers are often rejected. The responder may sacrifice money to punish an offer perceived as unfair. That behaviour is consistent with reciprocity, social norms or inequality aversion.','This is useful because it shows how experimental economics can reveal preferences rather than assuming them.'],checkpoint:'I can explain why rejecting a positive offer can be rational under social preferences.'},
      {kicker:'pareto criterion',title:'4. Pareto efficiency',body:['An allocation is Pareto efficient when there is no technically feasible alternative that makes at least one person better off without making anyone worse off.','Notice what this does not say. Pareto efficiency does not mean equal, kind, just or desirable. A highly unequal allocation can be Pareto efficient if every feasible redistribution hurts someone.','Likewise, an inefficient outcome means there is at least one unexploited mutual gain.'],callout:'Do not write “Pareto efficient = everyone is better off.” The correct test is whether a Pareto improvement remains available.',checkpoint:'I can test an allocation for Pareto efficiency using the definition.'},
      {kicker:'nash vs pareto',title:'5. Nash equilibrium and Pareto efficiency answer different questions',body:['Nash equilibrium asks whether anyone wants to change strategy unilaterally given the strategies chosen by others. Pareto efficiency asks whether there is another feasible allocation that could improve at least one person without harming anyone.','A Nash equilibrium may be Pareto efficient or Pareto inefficient. The prisoner’s dilemma is the obvious example of a Nash equilibrium that leaves mutual gains unrealised.','This distinction is one of the highest-value conceptual checks in the game-theory block.'],checkpoint:'I will not use Nash equilibrium and Pareto efficiency as synonyms.'},
      {kicker:'fairness',title:'6. Substantive versus procedural fairness',body:['Substantive fairness evaluates the characteristics of the final allocation—such as how unequal income, wealth or wellbeing is.','Procedural fairness evaluates how the allocation came about. Was participation voluntary? Was there discrimination? Was there equal opportunity? Did the rules recognise need or deservingness?','Two identical distributions can therefore be judged differently if one arose from coercion and the other from a process regarded as legitimate.'],checkpoint:'I can separately evaluate the outcome and the process that produced it.'},
      {kicker:'veil of ignorance',title:'7. The Rawlsian veil of ignorance as a fairness thought experiment',body:['The lecture introduces the veil of ignorance as background. Imagine choosing social rules without knowing whether you will be rich or poor, healthy or ill, advantaged or disadvantaged.','Removing knowledge of your own future position encourages an impartial judgement of institutions and rules. The purpose is not to give a mechanical answer; it is to make you interrogate whether a rule seems fair only because you know where you personally sit.'],checkpoint:'I can use the veil of ignorance to discuss a policy without treating it as a numerical formula.'},
      {kicker:'GTP method',title:'8. How to turn a real issue into a game-theory analysis',body:['The lecture’s AI-driving example and tutorial’s panic-buying, social-media and strike activities show the expected workflow. Start with a real problem, define the players and strategies, assign and justify numerical payoffs, solve the game, then discuss preferences and fairness.','The important skill is not inventing arbitrary numbers. The ordinal ranking of payoffs must match the facts and assumptions in the scenario. You must explain what each quadrant or branch represents.','For a sequential version, specify who moves first and reason from the later player’s likely response. Then evaluate the resulting allocation using efficiency and fairness.'],flow:['read scenario','define players','define strategies','justify payoffs','solve','interpret equilibrium','evaluate efficiency','evaluate fairness'],checkpoint:'I can reproduce the GTP-style reasoning chain on a fresh scenario.'},
      {kicker:'power and rules',title:'9. Changing the rules can change the equilibrium',body:['Policies, bargaining institutions, enforceable agreements and sequencing can alter payoffs or available actions. That can move a game from one equilibrium to another.','The strike exercise makes power explicit: the proposer and responder can have different outside options, patience and bargaining strength. Fairness and efficiency cannot be judged properly without noticing those features.'],checkpoint:'I can explain how institutions affect behaviour by changing the game rather than magically changing people.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'Can an allocation be Pareto efficient but very unequal?',answer:'Yes. Pareto efficiency only asks whether someone can be made better off without making anyone worse off. It does not impose equality.'},
      {q:'Why is rejection in the ultimatum game evidence against a purely self-regarding model?',answer:'Because a purely self-regarding responder should accept any positive amount; rejection suggests fairness norms, reciprocity or inequality aversion can enter preferences.'},
      {q:'What is the key analytical difference between a simultaneous game and a sequential game?',answer:'In a sequential game later players observe earlier moves, so strategies can be contingent on history and game-tree/backward reasoning is appropriate.'},
      {q:'For a GTP-style question, what must come after finding the equilibrium?',answer:'Interpret the result in the real-world context and evaluate the allocation, including efficiency, preferences/social norms, power and fairness where relevant.'}
    ],
    finish:['I can analyse a game tree.','I can distinguish Nash equilibrium from Pareto efficiency.','I can explain substantive and procedural fairness.','I can build and justify a GTP-style payoff structure from a real issue.','I attempted at least one L2(a)/L2(b) applied scenario.']
  });

  add('econ3', {
    unit:'ECON1000', date:'10–16 Aug', week:'4', duration:'120–180 min',
    title:'L3 · Economic Fluctuations',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin ECON1000 L3 Economic Fluctuations lecture deck plus the L3 workshop guide activity using FRED, nominal versus real GDP, recession identification and consumption-smoothing scenarios.',
    objectives:['Explain the business cycle and connect output changes to unemployment and wellbeing.','Measure GDP using spending, production/value-added and income approaches.','Use C + I + G + (X − M) to interpret GDP changes.','Distinguish nominal from real GDP.','Explain household consumption smoothing and its limits.','Explain why investment is more volatile than consumption.','Define inflation and CPI at the introductory level used in L3.'],
    map:['GDP measurement','business cycle','household shocks','consumption smoothing','investment expectations','aggregate-demand volatility','inflation preview'],
    sections:[
      {kicker:'business cycle',title:'1. What is actually fluctuating?',body:['The business cycle is the movement between periods of stronger and weaker economic growth. A recession is a period where output is declining or below its normal level.','Curtin links output to labour-market outcomes: when output falls, unemployment tends to rise and wellbeing can fall. This is why GDP growth is not just an abstract statistic.'],flow:['shock','output / GDP changes','employment changes','household and firm behaviour changes','further economic effects'],checkpoint:'I can describe a recession in terms of output and connect it to labour-market outcomes.'},
      {kicker:'national accounts',title:'2. Three equivalent ways to measure GDP',body:['National accounts measure overall output and expenditure in an economy. The lecture emphasises that GDP can be measured three equivalent ways: total spending on domestic products, total domestic production measured as value added, or total domestic income.','The circular-flow logic explains the equivalence. Spending on domestically produced output becomes revenue to producers, which is ultimately distributed as income or retained within production accounts.'],checkpoint:'I can explain why spending, output and income approaches are measuring the same underlying activity.'},
      {kicker:'expenditure components',title:'3. C + I + G + (X − M)',body:['The spending approach decomposes GDP into consumption, investment, government purchases and net exports. This decomposition helps identify which part of demand is driving a boom or downturn.','Consumption is household spending on goods and services. Investment includes firms’ equipment and structures, residential construction and changes in inventories. Government spending is government purchases of goods and services. Net exports are exports minus imports.','A component can be a small share of GDP yet create a large change in GDP if it is especially volatile. The lecture notes that investment can have an outsized role in downturns.'],formulas:['GDP = C + I + G + (X − M)'],checkpoint:'I can classify a transaction into the correct expenditure component.'},
      {kicker:'real vs nominal',title:'4. Nominal GDP versus real GDP',body:['GDP is measured in money terms, so price changes can make nominal GDP rise even when physical production has not risen by the same amount. Real GDP adjusts for price changes so that changes over time more closely reflect changes in output volume.','The tutorial uses FRED to compare nominal and real GDP and then transforms real GDP into percentage changes to identify downturns. The broader skill is learning to interrogate a macroeconomic series rather than reading a level mechanically.'],callout:'When comparing output across time, ask whether the series is nominal/current-price or real/constant-price.',checkpoint:'I can explain why nominal GDP growth can overstate growth in real production when prices rise.'},
      {kicker:'household shocks',title:'5. Shocks, self-insurance and co-insurance',body:['A shock is an unexpected event that can alter income and spending. Households try to avoid large swings in consumption because consumption directly affects living standards.','Self-insurance uses saving and borrowing. Co-insurance uses support from other people or government. Both can reduce how strongly current consumption moves with a temporary income shock.'],bullets:['Self-insurance: saving in good times or borrowing/drawing down savings in bad times.','Co-insurance: family, social networks, insurance arrangements or government support sharing the shock.'],checkpoint:'I can distinguish self-insurance from co-insurance.'},
      {kicker:'life cycle',title:'6. Consumption smoothing over a lifetime',body:['The life-cycle model treats consumption as based partly on expected lifetime resources rather than only current income. A temporary shock should therefore have a smaller effect on long-run consumption than a permanent shock.','If an earlier promotion raises lifetime expected income, a household may increase consumption before the higher income physically arrives by borrowing or saving less. If a temporary repair bill occurs, a household may borrow or use savings so that ordinary consumption does not collapse.'],checkpoint:'I can explain why temporary and permanent income shocks should affect consumption differently.'},
      {kicker:'limits',title:'7. Why consumption smoothing can fail',body:['Credit constraints limit borrowing, so some households cannot shift future income into the present. Present bias can make households fail to save even when they understand that future income will fall. Limited co-insurance can leave a household exposed to a shock.','These limits matter at the macro level because if many households are forced to cut spending at once, the initial shock can be amplified rather than smoothed.'],checkpoint:'I can identify a mechanism that causes household behaviour to amplify an economic downturn.'},
      {kicker:'investment',title:'8. Why investment is volatile',body:['Firms’ investment decisions depend heavily on expected future demand and expected profitability. High demand increases capacity utilisation and can encourage investment, which itself adds to aggregate demand.','Investment is more volatile than consumption because firms do not generally try to smooth investment for its own sake. When expectations shift, many firms can revise plans together. Business confidence can therefore coordinate a boom or a sharp pullback.','The lecture links this logic to financial crises and the Great Recession, where falls in investment and other components of aggregate demand contributed strongly to falling output.'],flow:['expected demand rises','capacity utilisation rises','investment rises','aggregate demand rises further'],checkpoint:'I can explain why investment can amplify the business cycle.'},
      {kicker:'inflation preview',title:'9. CPI and inflation before the deeper L7 treatment',body:['Inflation is an increase in the general average price level, not simply a rise in one product’s price.','The CPI tracks the prices of a representative basket of goods and services and weights categories according to their importance in household spending. L3 introduces this measurement; L7 returns to inflation in much greater depth.'],checkpoint:'I can distinguish a relative price increase from economy-wide inflation.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'Why are imports subtracted in the expenditure formula?',answer:'Because C, I and G can include spending on imported goods, but GDP measures domestic production. Subtracting imports removes foreign-produced output from the total.'},
      {q:'A worker receives reliable news that their salary will permanently rise next month. What does consumption smoothing predict?',answer:'They may increase consumption before the salary actually rises, using borrowing or reduced saving, because expected lifetime resources increased.'},
      {q:'Why can investment fall much more sharply than consumption in a recession?',answer:'Investment is highly sensitive to expected future demand and profit, while households often try to smooth consumption across temporary shocks.'},
      {q:'What does a real GDP series allow you to do better than a nominal GDP series?',answer:'Compare changes in production/output over time without confusing them with changes in the general price level.'}
    ],
    finish:['I can decompose GDP.','I understand real versus nominal GDP.','I can explain consumption smoothing and its limits.','I can explain investment volatility.','I worked through the FRED-style GDP/recession logic from the tutorial guide.']
  });

  add('econ4', {
    unit:'ECON1000', date:'24–30 Aug', week:'6', duration:'150–210 min',
    title:'L4 · Aggregate Demand and Fiscal Policy',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin ECON1000 L4 Aggregate Demand and Fiscal Policy lecture deck plus the S2 2026 workshop questions on the multiplier, Australian fiscal stimulus and austerity/spending cuts.',
    objectives:['Build the consumption function and interpret autonomous consumption and MPC.','Explain goods-market equilibrium using the AD and 45-degree lines.','Explain the multiplier process.','Connect wealth, credit constraints, saving and investment to aggregate demand.','Add government and net exports to the AD model.','Distinguish automatic stabilisers from discretionary fiscal policy.','Explain the paradox of thrift and why balanced-budget thinking can be destabilising in recession.'],
    map:['consumption function','aggregate demand','45° equilibrium','shock','multiplier','fiscal stabilisation','new equilibrium'],
    sections:[
      {kicker:'consumption function',title:'1. Autonomous consumption and the marginal propensity to consume',body:['Aggregate consumption is modelled with an autonomous part plus a part that rises with income. Autonomous consumption captures spending not mechanically driven by current income; expectations and wealth can shift it.','The marginal propensity to consume (MPC) is the slope of the consumption function: how much consumption rises when income rises by one additional unit. Credit-constrained households often have a higher MPC because current income matters more to what they can spend now.'],formulas:['C = c₀ + c₁Y','c₁ = marginal propensity to consume (MPC)'],checkpoint:'I can explain the intercept and slope of the consumption function.'},
      {kicker:'goods market',title:'2. Aggregate demand and the 45-degree line',body:['In the simplified model, aggregate demand begins with consumption plus investment. Investment is initially treated as autonomous with respect to current output.','The 45-degree line contains all points where output equals aggregate demand. Goods-market equilibrium occurs at the intersection of the AD line and the 45-degree line.','If planned demand is above current output, firms have an incentive to expand production. If planned demand is below output, inventories accumulate and firms cut production.'],formulas:['AD = c₀ + c₁Y + I','Equilibrium: Y = AD'],checkpoint:'I can explain why the intersection is an equilibrium rather than just a graph crossing.'},
      {kicker:'multiplier',title:'3. The multiplier process',body:['Suppose investment falls. The immediate effect is lower aggregate demand. But the story does not end there: lower spending means lower income for someone else, which reduces their consumption, which reduces somebody else’s income again.','The total decline in output can therefore exceed the initial decline in autonomous demand. The strength of this amplification depends heavily on the MPC in the simplified model.','The tutorial explicitly compares fiscal scenarios with different MPCs: a larger MPC produces a larger multiplier because more of each additional round of income is spent again.'],formulas:['Simple multiplier = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC)','ΔY = multiplier × change in autonomous demand'],checkpoint:'I can narrate the multiplier as successive rounds of spending and income, not just quote the formula.'},
      {kicker:'wealth and credit',title:'4. Why housing wealth and credit constraints can move consumption',body:['House prices can affect consumption through household wealth and borrowing capacity. Higher home equity can raise perceived wealth or make borrowing easier. Lower house values can reduce collateral and tighten credit constraints.','Target wealth is the level of wealth a household aims to maintain. If expected future earnings or asset values fall, households may increase precautionary saving and cut consumption to rebuild wealth.','When many households do this together during a downturn, aggregate demand can fall further.'],checkpoint:'I can connect a fall in asset prices to a possible shift in autonomous consumption.'},
      {kicker:'investment',title:'5. Interest rates, expected profit and investment',body:['A firm compares the return from investment with alternative uses of funds and with the owner’s preferences over present versus future returns.','The lecture emphasises that a lower interest rate makes investment more attractive, while a higher expected profit rate shifts investment upward. Investment therefore responds both to financial conditions and to expectations about demand and profitability.'],checkpoint:'I can explain why a lower interest rate can increase investment, holding expected profit constant.'},
      {kicker:'full AD',title:'6. Adding government and net exports',body:['The broader expenditure identity is AD = C + I + G + NX, where NX is exports minus imports. Taxes affect disposable income and therefore consumption; imports leak spending away from domestic production.','For the ECON1000 multiplier diagram, the lecture simplifies by focusing on C, I, G and X and disregarding some tax/import detail. Know both the broad logic and the simplified classroom model.'],formulas:['AD = C + I + G + NX','NX = X − M'],checkpoint:'I can explain where government spending and net exports enter aggregate demand.'},
      {kicker:'stabilisers',title:'7. Automatic stabilisers versus discretionary fiscal policy',body:['Automatic stabilisers respond without a new policy decision. A proportional tax system collects less tax when income falls, while unemployment benefits and some transfers rise when people lose work. Both soften changes in disposable income and demand.','Discretionary fiscal policy is an active decision to change taxes, transfers or government spending to offset a shock. A fiscal stimulus can shift aggregate demand upward in recession.','The lecture’s key warning is symmetry: stabilising stimulus in a recession should generally be reversed as conditions normalise so that debt does not simply compound indefinitely.'],checkpoint:'I can classify a policy as automatic or discretionary and explain the transmission to AD.'},
      {kicker:'paradox of thrift',title:'8. Why everyone saving more can reduce total income',body:['For one household, cutting spending to repair its balance sheet can be sensible. For the whole economy, however, one person’s spending is another person’s income.','If many households simultaneously reduce spending in recession, aggregate demand falls, output falls and aggregate income can fall. This is the paradox of thrift and an example of the fallacy of composition: what is sensible for one part is not necessarily sensible for the whole.'],checkpoint:'I can explain the paradox of thrift without saying that saving is always bad.'},
      {kicker:'budget balance',title:'9. Why balancing the government budget every year can destabilise a recession',body:['During recession, tax revenues tend to fall while some government payments rise, pushing the budget toward deficit automatically.','If government then raises taxes or cuts spending solely to eliminate that deficit immediately, it can reduce aggregate demand further and deepen the downturn. This is why the lecture rejects a rule that the budget must always be balanced year by year.','The workshop’s austerity exercise asks you to use the same multiplier logic to explain how spending cuts can propagate through industries and household incomes.'],checkpoint:'I can explain why austerity can amplify a negative demand shock.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'If MPC = 0.75, what is the simple multiplier?',answer:'1 ÷ (1 − 0.75) = 4.'},
      {q:'Why would the multiplier often be larger for more credit-constrained households?',answer:'Because their MPC out of current income is typically higher, so more of each new dollar of income is spent in the next round.'},
      {q:'Give one automatic stabiliser and explain its effect in recession.',answer:'Unemployment benefits rise as unemployment rises, supporting household disposable income and consumption, which reduces the size of the fall in AD.'},
      {q:'Why can a spending cut intended to improve the government budget worsen activity in the short run?',answer:'The cut directly reduces AD and then triggers multiplier rounds of lower income and consumption, reducing output and potentially tax revenue as well.'}
    ],
    finish:['I can draw and narrate the 45-degree multiplier model.','I understand MPC and the multiplier.','I can distinguish automatic stabilisers from discretionary fiscal policy.','I can explain paradox of thrift and austerity through aggregate demand.','I attempted the Australian fiscal-policy workshop logic.']
  });

  add('econ5', {
    unit:'ECON1000', date:'31 Aug–6 Sep', week:'7', duration:'150–210 min',
    title:'L5 · Supply Side Model: Labour and Product Markets',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin ECON1000 L5 Supply Side Model lecture deck and the S2 2026 L5 workshop activities on unemployment measurement, the Sahm rule and WS/PS analysis.',
    objectives:['Measure unemployment, participation and underutilisation carefully.','Explain the real wage.','Derive the logic of the wage-setting curve.','Derive the logic of the price-setting curve and markup.','Find labour-market equilibrium where WS and PS intersect.','Explain involuntary unemployment in the model.','Analyse shifts caused by benefits, firing costs, productivity and competition.'],
    map:['labour-force measurement','real wage','wage setting','price setting','WS/PS intersection','structural unemployment','policy / productivity shifts'],
    sections:[
      {kicker:'measurement',title:'1. Who counts as employed, unemployed or out of the labour force?',body:['The labour market begins with classification. The unemployed are not in paid employment or self-employment, are available for work and are actively seeking work.','The lecture notes the ABS convention that even one hour of work in the reference week can classify a person as employed. A person with no job must satisfy job-search and availability criteria to count as unemployed.','This is why the headline unemployment rate is not a complete measure of labour slack. Underemployment and participation also matter.'],checkpoint:'I can explain why someone without a job may not be counted as unemployed.'},
      {kicker:'rates',title:'2. Unemployment rate, participation and underutilisation',body:['The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the labour force, not by the whole population. The participation rate measures the share of working-age people in the labour force.','Two countries can have the same unemployment rate but different employment outcomes if participation differs. Underutilisation adds underemployment to unemployment and can reveal spare labour capacity missed by the headline rate.'],formulas:['Unemployment rate = Unemployed ÷ Labour force × 100','Participation rate = Labour force ÷ Working-age population × 100'],checkpoint:'I can calculate and interpret unemployment and participation separately.'},
      {kicker:'real wage',title:'3. Why the model uses real wages',body:['The real wage is the nominal wage divided by the price level. It measures what a worker can actually buy rather than merely the dollar amount in the pay packet.','Workers care about purchasing power, while firms care about wages relative to productivity and prices. The WS/PS model therefore expresses equilibrium in real-wage terms.'],formulas:['Real wage = W ÷ P'],checkpoint:'I can explain how a nominal pay rise can fail to raise the real wage if prices rise equally fast.'},
      {kicker:'wage setting',title:'4. Wage-setting curve: the wage needed to motivate effort',body:['The wage-setting curve gives the real wage necessary at each employment/unemployment level to motivate workers to work hard and well.','When unemployment is low, losing a job is less costly because another job is easier to find. Employers therefore need to pay a higher no-shirking wage to maintain effort. This gives the WS curve its upward relationship with employment and downward relationship with unemployment.','Factors such as more generous unemployment benefits, greater difficulty firing a shirking worker, or higher disutility of work can shift the WS curve upward.'],checkpoint:'I can explain both the slope of WS and at least two reasons it can shift.'},
      {kicker:'price setting',title:'5. Price-setting curve: firms, marginal cost and markup',body:['Firms pay a nominal wage and set product prices to maximise profit. The profit margin is price minus marginal cost, and the markup is the margin as a proportion of price.','The markup determines how revenue is split between labour costs and profits. Given labour productivity and markup, the price-setting decision implies a real wage firms can sustain.','Higher productivity can shift the PS curve upward because more output is produced per worker. More product-market competition can reduce the markup, lowering prices relative to wages and increasing the real wage.'],formulas:['Profit margin = P − MC','Markup μ = (P − MC) ÷ P'],checkpoint:'I can explain why higher productivity or lower markup can raise the PS real wage.'},
      {kicker:'equilibrium',title:'6. Where WS and PS meet',body:['Labour-market equilibrium is where the wage-setting and price-setting curves intersect. At this point firms have set profit-maximising prices and workers receive the real wage needed to motivate effort at that employment level.','The corresponding unemployment rate is structural in the model: it is not simply a short-run recession gap. Different institutions and productivity/competition conditions can therefore generate different equilibrium unemployment rates across countries.'],checkpoint:'I can read an intersection and identify both equilibrium real wage and employment/unemployment.'},
      {kicker:'involuntary unemployment',title:'7. Why the model contains unemployment even in equilibrium',body:['The lecture emphasises involuntary unemployment. If unemployment were zero, the cost of job loss would be very low. The discipline mechanism that motivates effort would weaken.','Some unemployment therefore appears in equilibrium because employers need job loss to carry a cost. This is a model result, not a claim that unemployment is morally desirable.'],callout:'Do not confuse “equilibrium unemployment exists in this model” with “policy should maximise unemployment”.',checkpoint:'I can explain the incentive logic behind involuntary unemployment in the WS/PS model.'},
      {kicker:'comparative analysis',title:'8. Shifting WS and PS to explain different labour-market outcomes',body:['A higher unemployment benefit can shift WS upward. Stronger employment protection can also alter the cost of job loss and bargaining environment.','Higher productivity shifts PS upward. More product-market competition lowers markups and can shift PS upward as well.','To analyse a policy or shock, identify which curve changes, which direction it shifts, then read the new equilibrium real wage and unemployment rate. The workshop asks you to apply this approach to post-COVID labour-market changes.'],flow:['identify shock','WS or PS?','direction of shift','new intersection','real wage effect','unemployment effect'],checkpoint:'I can analyse a shock with the model instead of memorising a list of outcomes.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'Why can the unemployment rate fall even if the number of employed people barely changes?',answer:'If people leave the labour force, the denominator and the number counted as unemployed can fall, lowering the unemployment rate without much employment growth.'},
      {q:'What happens to the PS curve if productivity rises, all else equal?',answer:'It shifts upward: higher output per worker allows a higher real wage for a given markup.'},
      {q:'Why is the WS curve higher when unemployment is lower?',answer:'Job loss is less costly when alternative work is easier to find, so firms need a higher wage to motivate effort.'},
      {q:'What is the first question to ask when given a new labour-market policy?',answer:'Does it affect wage-setting incentives/bargaining (WS) or productivity/markup/price-setting (PS), and in which direction?'}
    ],
    finish:['I can calculate labour-market rates.','I understand the real wage.','I can derive WS and PS conceptually.','I can analyse shifts and structural unemployment.','I attempted the L5 WS/PS tutorial application.']
  });

  add('econ6', {
    unit:'ECON1000', date:'7–13 Sep', week:'8', duration:'150–210 min',
    title:'L6 · Unemployment and Inequality',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin ECON1000 L6 Unemployment and Inequality lecture deck plus the S2 2026 L6 workshop questions on Lorenz curves, Gini coefficients, WS/PS recovery and the common-pool fish game.',
    objectives:['Interpret and calculate the Gini coefficient conceptually.','Draw and interpret a Lorenz curve.','Distinguish market income from disposable income and explain redistribution.','Explain wage share versus profit share.','Connect markups, competition and inequality.','Explain how unions affect wages, unemployment and worker voice in the WS/PS framework.','Apply inequality concepts to distributional and common-pool problems.'],
    map:['distribution of income/wealth','Lorenz curve','Gini','taxes + transfers','wage/profit shares','unions + bargaining','WS/PS outcomes'],
    sections:[
      {kicker:'inequality',title:'1. What are we trying to measure?',body:['Inequality asks how unevenly income, wealth or another resource is distributed across people. A single rich/poor ratio throws away most of the distribution, so Curtin introduces the Lorenz curve and Gini coefficient to use information across the population.','The goal is not only to compute a number. You need to interpret what a higher or lower number says about the distribution.'],checkpoint:'I can state what dimension of inequality I am measuring before using a Gini.'},
      {kicker:'gini',title:'2. The Gini coefficient',body:['The Gini coefficient runs from 0 for perfect equality toward 1 for extreme inequality. The more unequal the distribution, the larger the Gini.','The lecture gives a small numerical example based on average pairwise differences relative to average income and also presents the geometric Lorenz-curve interpretation.','For a Lorenz diagram, the Gini is the area between the perfect-equality line and the Lorenz curve divided by the total area beneath the equality line.'],formulas:['Gini ≈ A ÷ (A + B) on the Lorenz diagram'],checkpoint:'I can interpret 0, a middle value and a value close to 1.'},
      {kicker:'lorenz',title:'3. Building and reading a Lorenz curve',body:['Line the population up from poorest to richest on the horizontal axis. On the vertical axis plot the cumulative share of the resource they own or income they receive.','Perfect equality lies on the 45-degree line: for example, the poorest 40% would receive 40% of income. A Lorenz curve bowed farther below the equality line indicates greater inequality.','The tutorial asks you to compare stylised land distributions. This is exactly the skill: order groups, calculate cumulative population shares and cumulative resource shares, then compare the curves.'],checkpoint:'I can construct cumulative shares and explain which of two Lorenz curves is more unequal.'},
      {kicker:'redistribution',title:'4. Market income versus disposable income',body:['Market income includes wages, salaries, self-employment, business and investment income before direct taxes and cash transfers. Disposable income is what remains after direct taxes plus government cash transfers.','Progressive taxation and transfers can reduce inequality, so a country’s disposable-income Gini can be substantially lower than its market-income Gini. The size of the difference reflects the redistributive effect of policy.'],flow:['market income','subtract direct taxes','add cash transfers','disposable income'],checkpoint:'I can explain why market Gini and disposable Gini differ.'},
      {kicker:'functional distribution',title:'5. Wage share, profit share and markups',body:['Inequality is also influenced by how output is split between workers and owners of firms. The lecture highlights profit share versus wage share.','If product-market competition declines and markups rise, more revenue can flow toward profits relative to labour compensation. That can contribute to higher inequality depending on who owns firms and how income is distributed.','This links L6 back to the L5 price-setting curve: markup is not only a pricing parameter; it also affects the distribution of income.'],checkpoint:'I can connect a higher markup to the wage/profit split and then to possible inequality effects.'},
      {kicker:'unions',title:'6. Wage bargaining and the bargaining curve',body:['Where workers organise, wages may be negotiated rather than set unilaterally by the employer. A union can threaten strike action, giving workers bargaining power.','The bargained wage can lie above the ordinary wage-setting curve. In the simple long-run diagram this can create higher structural unemployment without necessarily raising the long-run real wage for those employed, because the PS constraint still matters.','But the lecture also warns that real-world evidence is more complicated than a one-direction story.'],checkpoint:'I can explain the simple model prediction without claiming it perfectly describes all unionised economies.'},
      {kicker:'inclusive unions',title:'7. Why union effects are not mechanically “higher wages = higher unemployment”',body:['Inclusive unions represent many workers and sectors and may internalise the long-run employment effects of very high wage demands. They may therefore exercise bargaining power differently from a narrow group.','This helps explain why cross-country data do not show a simple one-to-one relationship between union coverage and unemployment. Institutions matter.'],checkpoint:'I can explain why the empirical relationship between unionisation and unemployment can differ from the simplest diagram.'},
      {kicker:'voice effect',title:'8. The union voice effect',body:['The union voice effect is the possibility that unions improve worker treatment, participation and job satisfaction. If work becomes less unpleasant, the wage required to motivate effort can fall.','In WS/PS terms, this can shift the relevant wage-setting/bargaining relationship downward, potentially improving employment outcomes. The lecture uses this to show that unions can have both bargaining and productivity/effort effects.'],checkpoint:'I can distinguish the wage-bargaining effect from the union-voice effect.'},
      {kicker:'golden age',title:'9. Using WS/PS to interpret historical shifts',body:['The lecture applies WS/PS to the post-war “golden age” and its end. Technological progress can shift PS upward; stronger worker bargaining can shift WS upward.','Later productivity slowdown and oil shocks can push PS downward while strong wage claims create a bargaining gap. The important skill is the method: identify the economic mechanism, map it to a curve shift, then describe the resulting wage, unemployment and inflation pressure.'],checkpoint:'I can translate a historical narrative into WS/PS shifts.'},
      {kicker:'common pool',title:'10. The fish game: distribution and a social dilemma meet again',body:['The tutorial’s fishery is a common-pool resource. Each player has an incentive to harvest fish, but if the group harvests too aggressively the remaining stock may be too small to reproduce sustainably.','This revisits L1’s social-dilemma logic while adding distributional questions: who captures the resource, who bears depletion costs and how rules could alter behaviour.'],checkpoint:'I can connect the fish game to free riding/common-pool incentives and distributional outcomes.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'Which distribution is more unequal: a Lorenz curve close to the equality line or one bowed far below it?',answer:'The curve bowed farther below the equality line is more unequal and corresponds to a higher Gini coefficient.'},
      {q:'What is disposable income?',answer:'Income available after direct taxes and after adding government cash transfers.'},
      {q:'How can stronger product-market competition affect the PS curve and distribution?',answer:'Lower markups can raise the real wage implied by price setting and reduce profit share relative to wage share, all else equal.'},
      {q:'Why might a union reduce the wage needed to motivate workers in some workplaces?',answer:'Through the union voice effect—better treatment, representation and job satisfaction can reduce the disutility of effort.'}
    ],
    finish:['I can interpret Lorenz curves and Gini coefficients.','I understand market versus disposable income.','I can explain wage/profit share and markups.','I can analyse union effects with more nuance than one memorised shift.','I attempted the L6 distribution/WS-PS workshop tasks.']
  });

  add('econ7', {
    unit:'ECON1000', date:'14–20 Sep', week:'9', duration:'150–210 min',
    title:'L7 · Inflation and the Phillips Curve',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin ECON1000 L7 workshop guide, Adapted RBA Inflation Activity, the supplied L7 learning material, and the course sequence linking bargaining gaps, expected inflation and the Phillips Curve.',
    objectives:['Measure and interpret inflation as a change in the general price level.','Explain why different expenditure categories can experience different price changes.','Explain bargaining gaps in WS/PS terms.','Connect unemployment to inflation pressure through the Phillips Curve.','Explain how expected inflation can shift inflation dynamics.','Calculate and interpret approximate real interest rates.','Use the RBA-style inflation activity to connect data to monetary-policy reasoning.'],
    map:['CPI / price level','inflation rate','labour-market pressure','bargaining gap','Phillips Curve','expected inflation','policy response'],
    sections:[
      {kicker:'measurement',title:'1. Inflation is about the general price level',body:['Inflation is a sustained increase in the general average price level. A rise in the price of one good is a relative-price change, not automatically economy-wide inflation.','The CPI summarises price movements across a basket of consumer goods and services. Because households spend different shares on different categories, those categories receive different weights in the index.'],checkpoint:'I can explain why “petrol got more expensive” is not by itself a complete statement about inflation.'},
      {kicker:'basket',title:'2. Different prices move differently',body:['The adapted RBA activity deliberately compares price behaviour across expenditure groups such as fruit and telecommunications. Individual prices can rise, fall or move at different speeds even while the overall CPI rises.','A category’s influence on CPI depends on both its price movement and its weight in household expenditure. This is why inflation analysis should look beyond one memorable item.'],checkpoint:'I can explain how a heavily weighted category can matter more for CPI than a lightly weighted category with a larger percentage price change.'},
      {kicker:'inflation target',title:'3. Comparing observed inflation with a target',body:['The RBA-style exercise asks you to calculate average annual price changes over different periods and compare them with Australia’s inflation target. The learning objective is not to memorise one historical number; it is to interpret whether inflation is running below, within or above the central bank’s desired range and what that might imply for policy.'],checkpoint:'I can compare an observed inflation rate with a target and describe the policy problem created by persistent deviation.'},
      {kicker:'bargaining gap',title:'4. The bargaining gap connects WS/PS to inflation',body:['At supply-side equilibrium, workers’ wage claims and firms’ profit claims are mutually consistent with available output per worker. A bargaining gap appears when the combined claims are inconsistent.','In a recession with high unemployment, worker bargaining power is weaker and a negative bargaining gap can emerge, putting downward pressure on price growth. When unemployment is unusually low and bargaining pressure is strong, a positive gap can put upward pressure on prices and wages.'],checkpoint:'I can explain the sign of the bargaining gap from labour-market conditions.'},
      {kicker:'phillips curve',title:'5. Unemployment and inflation',body:['The Phillips Curve summarises a short-run relationship between unemployment and inflation pressure. Strong aggregate demand can reduce unemployment below its supply-side equilibrium, creating a positive bargaining gap and rising inflation. Weak demand can create the opposite.','The relationship is not a permanent menu where policymakers can pick any inflation/unemployment combination forever. Expectations and supply-side changes matter.'],flow:['AD rises','unemployment falls','bargaining gap becomes more positive','wage/price pressure rises','inflation rises'],checkpoint:'I can narrate the short-run Phillips Curve mechanism rather than merely say “they are negatively related”.'},
      {kicker:'expectations',title:'6. Why expected inflation matters',body:['If workers and firms come to expect higher inflation, wage bargains and price-setting behaviour can incorporate that expectation. The inflation process can therefore continue even without the same original demand shock.','This is why the course includes expected inflation as a factor that can shift the Phillips Curve relationship over time. Stable expectations make inflation easier to manage; unanchored expectations make the adjustment harder.'],checkpoint:'I can explain how expectations can change actual wage and price setting.'},
      {kicker:'negative shock',title:'7. Recession, a negative bargaining gap and the price-level path',body:['The workshop asks you to begin at stable prices, impose an investment decline and track a negative bargaining gap over several years.','The point is dynamic: if the negative gap persists, price-level growth is weaker and can become falling prices depending on the setup. You should be able to sketch the path and explain who may gain or lose from unexpected price changes.'],checkpoint:'I can trace a persistent negative bargaining gap through to a changing price level.'},
      {kicker:'interest rates',title:'8. Nominal versus real interest rates and inflation surprises',body:['The inflation-expectations game distinguishes nominal interest rates from real interest rates. A useful approximation is real interest rate equals nominal interest rate minus inflation.','Unexpected inflation redistributes between borrowers and lenders. Inflation higher than expected reduces the realised real interest rate, benefiting the borrower relative to the original contract and hurting the lender. Inflation lower than expected does the reverse.'],formulas:['Approximate real interest rate = nominal interest rate − inflation rate'],checkpoint:'I can identify who gains from inflation being higher or lower than expected in a fixed nominal loan.'},
      {kicker:'integrated analysis',title:'9. From an inflation observation to a policy discussion',body:['A good ECON1000 answer links data to a model. First identify what happened to inflation and unemployment. Then diagnose whether the change looks demand-driven, supply-driven or expectation-driven. Then use the Phillips Curve/WS-PS logic to discuss policy trade-offs.','The RBA activity’s final question—what monetary-policy response might be warranted—is therefore not a standalone opinion question. It should follow from the inflation evidence and the model.'],checkpoint:'I can move from an inflation chart to an economic explanation and then to a policy implication.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'Nominal interest is 7% and expected inflation is 3%. What real interest rate do borrower and lender expect?',answer:'Approximately 4%.'},
      {q:'If actual inflation is only 1% on that 7% nominal loan, who benefits relative to expectation?',answer:'The lender benefits and the borrower is worse off because the realised real interest rate is about 6%, higher than the 4% expected.'},
      {q:'What happens to inflation pressure if a positive AD shock pushes unemployment below supply-side equilibrium?',answer:'A positive bargaining gap tends to emerge, increasing wage and price pressure and pushing inflation upward.'},
      {q:'Why can inflation expectations make high inflation persistent?',answer:'Workers and firms incorporate expected inflation into wage demands and price-setting, which can reproduce inflation in subsequent periods.'}
    ],
    finish:['I understand CPI and the inflation rate.','I can explain bargaining gaps.','I can narrate Phillips Curve dynamics.','I understand expected inflation and real interest rates.','I worked through the logic of the RBA inflation activity and L7 workshop questions.']
  });

  add('econ8', {
    unit:'ECON1000', date:'21–27 Sep', week:'10', duration:'150–210 min',
    title:'L8 · Monetary Policy',
    sourceBasis:'Curtin ECON1000 L8 workshop guide and supplied L8 learning material covering monetary policy, the cash rate, inflation targeting, domestic transmission channels, the exchange-rate channel and the New Zealand policy application.',
    objectives:['Explain the role of monetary policy alongside fiscal policy.','Explain how a central-bank cash-rate decision affects market interest rates and aggregate demand.','Trace domestic transmission channels from rates to consumption and investment.','Explain the exchange-rate channel.','Connect monetary policy to the multiplier model and Phillips Curve.','Explain why monetary policy can be weak or constrained in some recessions.','Evaluate a tightening/easing episode using output, unemployment and inflation.'],
    map:['inflation/output problem','central-bank rate decision','financial conditions','C + I + NX','aggregate demand','unemployment','inflation'],
    sections:[
      {kicker:'role',title:'1. What monetary policy is trying to stabilise',body:['Monetary policy uses the central bank’s policy interest rate and related tools to influence aggregate demand and inflation. In the ECON1000 framework it sits beside fiscal policy as a macroeconomic stabilisation tool.','The key is transmission. The central bank does not directly order households to spend less or firms to invest more. It changes financial conditions, which alter private decisions and therefore aggregate demand.'],checkpoint:'I can describe monetary policy as a transmission process rather than “the bank changes inflation directly”.'},
      {kicker:'cash rate',title:'2. From the policy rate to borrowing and saving conditions',body:['A higher policy/cash rate tends to raise interest rates faced by households and firms; a lower rate tends to reduce them.','Higher rates make borrowing more expensive and saving more attractive, while lowering the present value of some investment projects. Lower rates generally work in the opposite direction.','The strength and speed of pass-through can vary across borrowers, banks, mortgage structures and economic conditions.'],checkpoint:'I can explain why a cash-rate move changes incentives even though the central bank does not set every retail interest rate one-for-one.'},
      {kicker:'consumption channel',title:'3. Household consumption channels',body:['Higher interest rates can reduce consumption by increasing debt-servicing costs for borrowers, encouraging saving and weakening asset prices/wealth. Lower rates can support consumption by easing financing and improving cash flow for some borrowers.','Distribution matters: borrowers and savers respond differently, and highly indebted households may react more strongly.'],checkpoint:'I can give more than one channel from interest rates to household consumption.'},
      {kicker:'investment channel',title:'4. Business and housing investment',body:['Recall L4: firms compare expected project returns with the cost of finance and alternative returns. A higher interest rate makes fewer projects profitable at the margin, reducing investment demand.','Lower rates can support business investment and housing activity, but only if firms and households are willing and able to borrow. Weak profit expectations can make rate cuts less effective.'],checkpoint:'I can connect the L4 investment function to monetary policy.'},
      {kicker:'exchange rate',title:'5. The exchange-rate channel and net exports',body:['Interest-rate changes can influence international capital flows and the exchange rate. A lower domestic interest rate can, all else equal, put downward pressure on the currency.','A lower currency makes domestic exports cheaper to foreigners and imports more expensive to domestic buyers, tending to raise net exports and aggregate demand. An appreciation tends to work in the opposite direction.','Exchange rates are affected by many factors, so this is a channel, not a guaranteed one-for-one rule.'],flow:['rate change','capital/asset demand changes','exchange rate changes','exports/imports respond','NX changes','AD changes'],checkpoint:'I can trace the exchange-rate channel all the way to aggregate demand.'},
      {kicker:'full transmission',title:'6. From monetary policy to inflation',body:['Combine the channels: a rate increase tends to weaken C, I and sometimes NX, shifting aggregate demand downward. Lower demand reduces output growth and raises unemployment relative to what it otherwise would have been.','Through the Phillips Curve/bargaining-gap mechanism, weaker labour-market pressure reduces inflation pressure with a lag. A rate cut reverses the direction when the goal is to support demand.'],flow:['cash rate ↑','C / I / NX ↓','AD ↓','output ↓','unemployment ↑','bargaining pressure ↓','inflation pressure ↓'],checkpoint:'I can integrate L4, L5 and L7 into one monetary-policy transmission story.'},
      {kicker:'inflation targeting',title:'7. Inflation targeting and expectations',body:['A credible inflation target helps anchor expectations. If households, workers and firms believe inflation will return toward target, they may be less likely to build very high future inflation into wages, prices and contracts.','This expectations channel matters because L7 showed that expected inflation can shift the inflation process itself. Monetary policy therefore works partly through current spending and partly through beliefs about future inflation and policy.'],checkpoint:'I can explain why credibility can affect inflation before every real-side channel has fully worked.'},
      {kicker:'limits',title:'8. Why rate cuts may be less powerful in a deep recession',body:['The workshop explicitly asks about limitations of monetary policy when trying to stimulate demand in a recession with persistent low inflation.','If households are heavily indebted, worried about jobs or credit constrained, cheaper borrowing may not trigger much new spending. Firms with weak expected demand may not invest even at low rates. Banks may tighten lending standards. Interest rates also cannot be reduced without practical limits.','This is why fiscal policy and monetary policy may need to work together in severe downturns.'],checkpoint:'I can explain at least three reasons an interest-rate cut might have a weak effect.'},
      {kicker:'case method',title:'9. Analysing a tightening cycle: the New Zealand-style workshop',body:['The L8 workshop asks you to begin with very low unemployment and a positive demand/inflation problem, then trace the effects of tighter monetary policy across several years.','A strong answer should use more than one diagram mentally: tighter policy shifts AD downward; the labour market weakens; unemployment rises; the bargaining gap shrinks or turns negative; inflation falls with a lag.','Then evaluate the socioeconomic trade-off. Lower inflation can restore price stability, but overly tight policy can generate recession, unemployment and lost output.'],checkpoint:'I can evaluate policy using both inflation and real-economy outcomes rather than declaring a rate rise simply “good” or “bad”.'},
      {kicker:'integration',title:'10. The full ECON macro chain',body:['By L8, the unit’s macroeconomics is one connected system. Households and firms create aggregate demand. Demand shocks are amplified by the multiplier. Fiscal and monetary policy influence demand. The WS/PS system determines supply-side labour-market equilibrium. Deviations from that equilibrium create bargaining pressure and inflation dynamics.','The final-assessment preparation will draw on this ability to combine models, data and real events.'],flow:['shock / policy','aggregate demand','multiplier','output','unemployment vs structural equilibrium','bargaining gap','inflation'],checkpoint:'I can tell one coherent story across L3–L8.'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {q:'Trace a cash-rate increase through to inflation using the ECON1000 model.',answer:'Higher rates weaken consumption/investment and possibly net exports, shifting AD down. Output and employment weaken, unemployment rises, bargaining pressure falls, and inflation pressure declines with a lag.'},
      {q:'Why might an interest-rate cut fail to produce much investment in a recession?',answer:'If firms expect weak future demand/profits, even cheap finance may not make new capacity worthwhile.'},
      {q:'What is the exchange-rate channel of an interest-rate cut?',answer:'Lower domestic rates can reduce demand for the currency, causing depreciation; exports become cheaper and imports dearer, tending to raise net exports and AD.'},
      {q:'What trade-off can arise when a central bank fights high inflation aggressively?',answer:'Inflation may fall, but weaker aggregate demand can cause lower output and higher unemployment, especially if policy becomes excessively restrictive.'}
    ],
    finish:['I can trace domestic and exchange-rate transmission channels.','I can connect monetary policy to AD, WS/PS and Phillips Curve logic.','I understand inflation targeting and expectations.','I can discuss limits of monetary policy.','I attempted the L8 New Zealand-style policy analysis.']
  });
})();